course: dictionary
lesson: 13343
title: Haemorrhage: blood and money
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A haemorrhage is a lot of bleeding, and it is serious. Context tells you this. «The nurse pressed hard on the cut to stop the haemorrhage.» Pressing hard, stopping it and a cut all point to heavy bleeding that needs help quickly.
explain: Haemorrhage can also be a verb, meaning to bleed heavily. Money can be a clue too. «The club is haemorrhaging money, so it may close.» Clubs do not bleed, so this means losing a lot of money very fast. Ask yourself: does this sentence make sense about blood, or about something else?
explain: Read the nearby words first. A brain haemorrhage is a serious health problem, and «The news said the club was haemorrhaging customers» means it was losing many customers quickly. When you swap in your meaning, check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Haemorrhage means a lot of bleeding from a broken blood vessel, inside or outside the body.
fact: A haemorrhage is a serious emergency: call 999 in the UK, 911 in the US, 000 in Australia or 112 in many countries.
fact: «Haemorrhaging» can mean losing money or people very fast, as in «haemorrhaging money».
fact: In American English, the spelling is «hemorrhage», but it means the same thing.
fact: A brain haemorrhage is a kind of stroke, so it is always a medical emergency.
exercise: «The nurse pressed hard on the cut to stop the haemorrhage.» What does haemorrhage mean? | A small itch on the skin | *A lot of bleeding | A fast run around the park | A kind of bandage
exercise: «The club is haemorrhaging money, so it may close.» What does haemorrhaging mean here? | Counting coins slowly | Bleeding heavily from a cut | Growing bigger each year | *Losing money very fast
exercise: The club is ___ money fast. Which word fits? | *haemorrhaging | haemorrhage | haemorrhaged | haemorrhages
test:
«He had an internal haemorrhage after the fall, so the doctors rushed him to hospital.» What is an internal haemorrhage? | *Serious bleeding inside the body | A bruise that fades in a week | A cold that lasts a day | A kind of plaster
Which spelling is the British one for this word? | hemorrhage | *haemorrhage | hemmorage | haemmorage
exam:
«The shop began to haemorrhage customers after the new road opened.» What does haemorrhage mean here? | *Lose many customers quickly | Bleed onto the floor | Open a new road | Sell a lot of bread
